#: manual/design/immut.md:1
msgid "immut Design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:3
msgid "`immut` is the value-oriented layer of `luna-poly`. It models polynomials, exponent vectors, and multivariate term collections as canonical values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:5
#: manual/design/mutable.md:5
msgid "Responsibilities"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:7
msgid "Re-export shared `core` capability traits through the `immut` facade."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:8
msgid "Provide dense univariate `DensePolynomial[A]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:9
msgid "Provide `ExponentVector` as a multivariate exponent key."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:10
msgid "Provide two multivariate representations: sorted term sequence `TermPolynomial[A]` and ordered-map `SparsePolynomial[A]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:11
msgid "Implement ordinary algebraic operations and `arithmetic.PowNatChecked` for polynomial types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:13
#: manual/design/mutable.md:47
msgid "Canonical Form"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:15
msgid "`DensePolynomial` stores coefficients in a core immutable vector, removes trailing zero coefficients, and represents zero as an empty vector."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:16
msgid "`ExponentVector` stores exponents in a core immutable vector, removes trailing zero exponents, and caches total degree."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:17
msgid "`TermPolynomial` stores terms in a core immutable vector, merges equal exponents, removes zero coefficients, and keeps terms sorted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:18
msgid "`SparsePolynomial` stores non-zero terms in a `SortedMap`; an empty map is zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:20
msgid "These rules are observable through `to_coefficients`, `to_terms`, `size`, `degree`, and equality."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:22
msgid "Value Semantics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:24
msgid "Public constructors copy input arrays. Update-like operations return new values, including:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:28
msgid "`+`, `-`, `*`, `scale`, and `pow`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:30
msgid "Callers may safely reuse old values after any operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:32
msgid "Representation Differences"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:34
msgid "`TermPolynomial` and `SparsePolynomial` describe the same mathematical objects but optimize different access patterns:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:36
msgid "`TermPolynomial` favors ordered traversal and batch algebraic operations over a canonical term sequence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:37
msgid "`SparsePolynomial` favors lookup by `ExponentVector`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:38
msgid "Convert explicitly with `SparsePolynomial::from_terms(term.to_terms())` or `TermPolynomial::from_terms(sparse.to_terms())`; conversion reapplies canonicalization without coupling implementation packages to each other."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:42
msgid "Capability Boundary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:44
msgid "Algorithms that only need shared behavior should use `UnivariatePolynomial`, `MultivariatePolynomial`, or `ContextualPolynomial` instead of matching on a concrete storage type. When an algorithm needs construction or algebraic operations in addition to queries, accept the corresponding `Type::ops()` record and call its function fields."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:50
msgid "`HasShape` and `PolynomialShape` provide the dimension layer expected by generic numeric code: univariate values report coefficient length, multivariate values report arity and term count, and contextual values report their `VariableContext` plus arity and term count. Shape compatibility is available through `PolynomialShape::is_compatible_with` and `compatible_checked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:57
msgid "The facade re-exports common `luna-generic` algebra traits (`Zero`, `One`, `AddMonoid`, `MulMonoid`, `Semiring`, `Ring`, `Field`, and `Num`) so callers can write polynomial algorithms with the same capability vocabulary used by `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:62
msgid "Checked methods return `None` for caller contract failures such as negative powers, negative exponent-vector indexes, incomplete indexed evaluation, or context mismatch. Convenience methods keep the existing aborting behavior."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:66
msgid "Substitution Boundary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:68
msgid "Context-aware substitution uses `Luna-Flow/type_theory` names as the shared semantic naming layer. `luna-poly` still owns polynomial semantics: canonicalization, coefficient arithmetic, powers, sparse/term storage, and context compatibility are handled inside `ContextPolynomial`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:73
msgid "Substitution is simultaneous and one pass. A variable can be replaced by a scalar coefficient or by a same-context polynomial, but inserted replacement polynomials are not recursively substituted again in the same call. Partial evaluation is the scalar-only specialization and preserves the original `VariableContext` instead of projecting away assigned variables. Checked substitution rejects foreign variables, duplicate replacement entries, unknown `type_theory` names, and polynomial replacements from incompatible contexts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:81
msgid "Maintenance Notes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:83
msgid "Document only APIs that exist on the current branch."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:84
msgid "Update this page when canonicalization, term ordering, zero handling, or evaluation abort conditions change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:85
msgid "Document any observable divergence from `mutable` in the corresponding `mutable` pages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:1
msgid "mutable Design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:3
msgid "`mutable` is the execution-oriented layer of `luna-poly`. It reuses the mathematical semantics and canonicalization rules of `immut`, while exposing explicitly mutating container operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:7
msgid "Re-export shared `core` capability traits through the `mutable` facade."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:8
msgid "Provide `DensePolynomial`, `TermPolynomial`, `SparsePolynomial`, and `ExponentVector` aligned with `immut`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:9
msgid "Provide `from_immut` and `to_immut` conversions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:10
msgid "Provide setters, `clear`, `copy`, and `_inplace` operations for incremental construction and updates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:11
msgid "Keep ordinary algebraic operators non-mutating."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:13
msgid "Mutation Boundary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:15
msgid "Public methods that mutate the receiver include:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:34
msgid "Other ordinary operators and methods such as `scale`, `pow`, and `substitute` return new values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:36
msgid "Relationship With immut"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:38
msgid "Many mutable operations convert through `immut` and then convert back. This keeps the two layers aligned on:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:40
msgid "trailing-zero removal for univariate polynomials,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:41
msgid "exponent-vector canonicalization and degree semantics,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:42
msgid "multivariate term merging and zero deletion,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:43
msgid "natural-number exponentiation and `arithmetic.PowNatChecked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:45
msgid "`mutable.ExponentVector` is a wrapper around `immut.ExponentVector`; it remains value-oriented so it can be used as a stable key."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:49
msgid "Mutating operations must restore canonical form before returning:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:51
msgid "`DensePolynomial` removes trailing zero coefficients."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:52
msgid "`TermPolynomial` merges duplicate terms and removes zero coefficients."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:53
msgid "`SparsePolynomial` removes map entries whose coefficients become zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:55
msgid "New in-place operations must preserve these invariants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:57
msgid "Usage Boundary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:59
msgid "Prefer `immut` by default. Use `mutable` when code needs step-by-step updates, fewer explicit intermediate values, or integration with an existing mutable algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:61
msgid "Generic code should depend on `UnivariatePolynomial`, `MultivariatePolynomial`, `ContextualPolynomial`, `MutablePolynomial`, or the relevant `Type::ops()` record instead of matching on a concrete mutable storage type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:65
msgid "Mutable containers also implement `HasShape`, so execution-oriented algorithms can inspect the same `PolynomialShape` metadata as immutable code. The mutable facade re-exports the `luna-generic` algebra traits used by the linear-algebra package, and mutable `Type::ops()` records expose construction, addition, multiplication, evaluation, scaling, and powers for generic algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:71
msgid "Checked methods mirror the immutable layer and return `None` for contract failures. Existing convenience methods remain aborting wrappers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:74
msgid "Mutable context substitution delegates to the immutable context model. Mutable wrappers expose the same scalar/polynomial substitution and partial-evaluation APIs, but the canonical result is produced by the immutable implementation and then wrapped back into `mutable.ContextPolynomial`. The same checked failure contracts apply: foreign variables, duplicate assignments, unknown `type_theory` names, and incompatible replacement polynomial contexts return `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:1
msgid "luna-poly"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:3
msgid "`luna-poly` provides canonical polynomial types for MoonBit with explicit immutable and mutable execution models. This manual describes version 0.2.0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:5
msgid "The library has one shared capability layer and two explicit execution models:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:7
msgid "`core` provides shared types, capability traits, and functional operation records."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:9
msgid "`immut` provides persistent value semantics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:10
msgid "`mutable` provides explicit setters and `_inplace` operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:12
msgid "Concrete implementations live in mirrored subpackages:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:17
msgid "The top-level `immut` and `mutable` packages are facades that re-export core capabilities plus their concrete implementations. Both facades expose dense univariate, sorted-term multivariate, and ordered-map sparse polynomials. Canonical form and natural-power behavior are shared across both packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:22
msgid "The 0.2.0 package layout is intentionally source-incompatible with the former root package. Import `/immut` or `/mutable` explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:24
msgid "Representations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:26
msgid "Both packages provide `DensePolynomial`, `TermPolynomial`, `SparsePolynomial`, `ExponentVector`, and context-aware polynomial helpers. Dense polynomials use ascending coefficient arrays; `TermPolynomial` uses a sorted term array; `SparsePolynomial` uses an ordered map. Every representation removes zero terms and maintains a canonical form."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:28
msgid "Natural powers implement `Luna-Flow/arithmetic.PowNatChecked`. The convenience `pow(UInt)` methods use the same semantics: exponent zero returns the multiplicative identity, including for the zero polynomial."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:30
msgid "Named variables"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:32
msgid "`Variable` and `VariableContext` provide the named-variable layer. A `ContextPolynomial` binds a variable context to either term-array or sparse storage, supports both indexed and named evaluation, and exposes checked variants for context or assignment failures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:34
msgid "Context-aware polynomials integrate with `Luna-Flow/type_theory` names for substitution-facing APIs. `Variable::to_type_theory_name` and `VariableContext::variable_by_type_theory_name` bridge polynomial variables to the shared semantic substrate. `ContextPolynomial::substitute_checked` replaces variables with scalars or same-context polynomials, while `eval_partial_checked` is the scalar-only partial-evaluation specialization. These operations preserve polynomial-owned canonicalization; `type_theory` provides the shared naming and substitution vocabulary, not polynomial storage or normalization."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:36
msgid "Generic code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:38
msgid "Use `UnivariatePolynomial`, `MultivariatePolynomial`, `ContextualPolynomial`, and `MutablePolynomial` when an algorithm only needs a capability boundary. Use `Type::ops()` records when functional-style generic code needs construction, conversion, or algebraic operations without matching on the storage representation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:44
msgid "The shared `core` layer also exposes lightweight shape metadata through `PolynomialShape` and `HasShape`. Generic code can inspect univariate length, multivariate arity and term count, or context compatibility before combining values. Checked APIs such as `coefficient_checked`, `scale_checked`, `eval_checked`, substitution, and context `*_checked` methods return `None` for contract failures; the shorter convenience methods abort instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:46
msgid "The `immut` and `mutable` facades re-export the common algebra traits from `Luna-Flow/luna-generic`, including `Zero`, `One`, `AddMonoid`, `MulMonoid`, `Semiring`, `Ring`, `Field`, and `Num`, so polynomial APIs can be used with the same capability style as `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra`."
msgstr ""

