msgid "Example"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:76
msgid "Where to go next"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:78
msgid "Start with the [`immut` tutorial](tutorial/immut.md) for value-style polynomials, or the [`mutable` tutorial](tutorial/mutable.md) for in-place updates. The design pages explain canonical form and representation invariants ([`immut`](design/immut.md)) and the mutation boundary ([`mutable`](design/mutable.md)). The generated `pkg.generated.mbti` files remain the authority for exact signatures. To work on the library itself, read the [contribution guidelines](contributing.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:1
msgid "immut Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:3
msgid "Use `immut` when polynomials should behave like ordinary values. Constructors copy input arrays, updates return new values, and old values remain unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:5
#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:5
msgid "Dense Univariate DensePolynomial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:14
msgid "`p` represents `1 + 2x + 3x^2`, so `value` is `17`. `substitute` does not mutate `p`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:16
msgid "Constructors keep canonical form:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:23
msgid "`coefficients` is `[1, 2]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:25
msgid "Exponent Vectors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:33
msgid "`xy2` represents `x * x_1^2`; its total degree is `3`. `x` is a new value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:35
#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:38
msgid "Multivariate Term Arrays"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:46
msgid "The first two terms have equivalent exponents and cancel out. The remaining term is `3 * x_1`, so `value` is `6`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:48
#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:48
msgid "Sparse Map Representation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:60
msgid "`p` represents `x^2 + 2x + 1`; `coeff` is `Some(2)` and `value` is `9`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:62
msgid "Choosing A Representation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:64
msgid "Use `DensePolynomial` for dense univariate polynomials."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:65
msgid "Use `TermPolynomial` for sequential multivariate term processing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:66
msgid "Use `SparsePolynomial` when exponent lookup matters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:67
msgid "Convert between multivariate representations explicitly: `SparsePolynomial::from_terms(term.to_terms())` or `TermPolynomial::from_terms(sparse.to_terms())`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/immut.md:70
msgid "Use `UnivariatePolynomial`, `MultivariatePolynomial`, `ContextualPolynomial`, or `Type::ops()` when generic code should not depend on the storage representation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:1
msgid "mutable Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:3
msgid "Use `mutable` when code needs explicit control over intermediate allocation or step-by-step construction. The mathematical semantics match `immut`, but mutation is limited to setters, `clear`, and `_inplace` methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:15
msgid "`snapshot` still represents `1 + 2x + 3x^2`. `p` is updated and then canonicalized to zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:17
msgid "Ordinary operators do not mutate the receiver:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:25
msgid "`q` is a new polynomial and `unchanged` is `[1, 2]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:27
msgid "Converting To And From immut"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:36
msgid "Conversions produce independent values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:46
msgid "The two exponent vectors canonicalize to the same key, so the terms cancel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:58
msgid "`coeff` is `Some(2)`. Setting a coefficient to zero removes the term."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:60
msgid "Guidance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:62
msgid "Use `_inplace` only when mutating the receiver is intentional."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:63
msgid "Prefer `immut` when code relies on sharing old values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/mutable.md:64
msgid "Use `to_immut` and `from_immut` at API boundaries to isolate mutable state."
msgstr ""
